Article 5
Every woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from discriminatory actions; b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
c. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain decent work in accordance with her capabilities; e. obtain work leave while receiving wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education;
g. obtain special protection in the implementation of her work or profession against matters that could threaten her safety and or health in relation to women's reproductive function; and
h. ownership and management of joint assets in marriage in accordance with statutory regulations.

Article 6
Every child has the right:
a. to live, grow, develop and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity; b. to obtain protection from acts of discrimination; c. to obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
d. to obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
e. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
f. to obtain a birth certificate free of charge; g. to embrace a religion and worship according to the teachings of their religion with the guidance of parents/guardians; h. to not be forced to change religion by foster parents, adoptive parents and by anyone;
i. to be recognized and know their parents, to be raised and cared for by their own parents;
j. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs. k. to obtain education and teaching in accordance with their interests and talents;
l. to obtain care, education, training, self-improvement and the ability to participate in community and state life for children with physical, mental disabilities and special needs;
m. to express and have their opinion heard; and n. to rest and utilize leisure time for self-development.

Article 3
Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to: a. serve as a guideline for local government in planning, policies and strategies for the protection of women and children;
b. protect women and children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and children; c. prevent violations of the rights of women and children;
d. increase empowerment of women and children;
e. enhance the role and value of local wisdom and the role of customs in the protection of women and children; and f. enhance the role of government and non-government institutions in the region in efforts to protect women and children.

Article 69
(1) The policy for the development of CFC is directed towards the fulfillment of Children's rights which are divided into 5 (five) groups, comprising: a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care; c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, utilization of leisure time, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.
(2) Further provisions regarding the policy for the development of CFC as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 24
The responsibility of the community to prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children includes:
a. monitoring various conditions that occur in their environment that may give rise to violations of the rights of women and children;
b. increasing awareness of violations of the rights of women and children;
c. strengthening the role of community organizations, religious organizations and the family environment; and
d. preventing unregistered marriages and marriage at child age.

1.10.text:
Article 23
(1) The responsibility of the Regional Government to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:
a. conducting socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women and children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children; c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in women's empowerment and child protection; and
g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women and children.
(2) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of: a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection; f. mental and spiritual;
g. informatics and communication; and h. public peace and order.
(3) Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their respective fields of duty in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

