7.1.text:
Article 3
Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to: a. serve as a guideline for local government in planning, policies and strategies for the protection of women and children;
b. protect women and children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and children; c. prevent violations of the rights of women and children;
d. increase empowerment of women and children;
e. enhance the role and value of local wisdom and the role of customs in the protection of women and children; and f. enhance the role of government and non-government institutions in the region in efforts to protect women and children.

Article 52
(1) Regional Government in efforts to fulfill the rights of Children is responsible for:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the fulfillment of Children's rights;
b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every Child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnic group, culture and language, legal status of the Child, birth order of the Child, and physical and/or mental condition;
c. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for improving family quality in realizing Children's rights;
d. guaranteeing the Protection, care, and welfare of Children with due regard to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for the Child;
e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of the fulfillment of Children's rights;
f. guaranteeing the right of Children to express opinions in accordance with the age and intelligence level of the Child;
g. conducting supervision and guidance in the context of implementing the fulfillment of Children's rights; and
h. conducting cooperation with private parties and/or NGOs in the implementation of Child Protection;
(2) The responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 70
(1) In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of KLA policy in the Region, a KLA Task Force is established.
(2) The KLA Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1), has the following main duties:
a. coordinating the implementation of policies and development of KLA;
b. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication of information and education on KLA policy;
c. collecting baseline data for KLA development; d. conducting needs analysis sourced from baseline data;
e. conducting dissemination of baseline data;
f. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing KLA, which are adjusted to the potential of the region;
g. preparing a 5 (five) year Child Friendly City regional action plan and work mechanism; and h. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least 1 (one) time per year.
(3) The establishment of the KLA Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.

